Z||000000||fiero56||03-22-2000||08:41 PM||||1988 Formula(v-6) 5-speed<BR>Several weeks ago I disconnected the drivers side parking brake cable to replace the seal between the caliper housing and the p. brake lever. I finished the job, but when I reconnected the cable, it would not equalize cable tension between the two calipers. The drivers side will never fully disenguage, and the passenger side doesn't fully enguage. How in the world do I get it to work properly? I have adjusted the equalizer nut, it adjusts cable tension, but doesn't equalize the tension between the two calipers. If I leave the parking brake system hooked up, the drivers side is always pulled tight, and the passenger side is always very slack. For the past several weeks I have been driving around without a parking brake, and it would be nice to have since I have a 5-speed. I have tried 4 or 5 times to fix this problem, nothing seems to work.<P>||||reg||5
Z||000001||fieroshop||03-22-2000||08:54 PM||||check out your cables that go the calypers. One might be frozen and needs to be replaced.||||reg||3
Z||000002||fiero56||03-22-2000||09:09 PM||||Guess I should have mentioned the cables all work fine. When I pull the parking brake handle, both cables tighten. But the drivers side pulls really tight, and the passenger side barely applies any pressure. Then, when released, the passenger side cable has about an inch of slack, and the drivers side still holds the rotor locked.||||reg||1
Z||000003||Phaeton||03-23-2000||12:08 AM||||My 88 did that after I rebuilt the brakes.  It doesn't take much extra friction to make one side drag.  If working the cables back and forth with lotsa lube running through doesn't help, well, the lever spring on the lagging side could be replaced with a stronger one.<BR>But I have to think that somehow sometime somewhere the diffent springs will weird something else out completely unrelated.||||reg||12
Z||000004||Oreif||03-23-2000||09:41 AM||||I'm not sure if this will work for an 88 but on my 86 the service manual says to  make sure both caliper pistons are full open then attach the E-brake levers. Then pump and hold the brakes with the brake pedal then pump the E-brake handle a couple of times. This is suppose to adjust the caliper pistons to E-brake cable and levers correctly. <p>[This message has been edited by Oreif (edited 03-23-2000).]||||reg||1
